  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 114 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nA large upper level trough off the West Coast is advertised by\nthe various ensemble suites to gradually move eastward over the\nnext several days, with deep tropical moisture up through the\nmid levels advecting northward. Upper level ridging will result\nin dry and warm weather through Friday, with highs in the upper\n70s to lower 80s. By Friday night the upper level ridge will \nprogress east of the high plains, allowing rich moisture to \nadvect northward into New Mexico and eastern Colorado. Any \nconvective instability will stay west of Kansas and rain is not\nforecast. \n\nBy Saturday, deep southerly flow will develop across the high\nplains, allowing for the tropical moisture to spread into\nwestern Kansas. Marginal surface based instability is expected \nto develop in far western Kansas. But the deep moisture, \ncloudiness and poor lapse rates are expected to discourage \nsevere weather, with storms marginally severe at best on \nSaturday. Instead, widespread showers and sub-severe t-storms \nwill gradually spread eastward. Clusters of showers moving \nnorthward across the same areas may result in locally heavy rain\nacross far western Kansas Saturday night and Sunday. The rain \nmay edge into central Kansas late Saturday night and early \nSunday; but drier air to the east will slow this process. For \nthe Saturday through Monday time period, the grand ensemble \nshows 60-80% chances for at least .5\" of rain and 40-60% chances\nof 1+\" of rain. This represents a strong signal that \nsignificant rains are likely across all of central and western \nKansas, with higher totals along and west of a line from Dodge \nCity to Hays. With the clouds and rain, highs Sunday may not get\nout of the 60s, with 50s likely for Monday in the wake of a \ncold front.",
